    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This paper presents the description of the fracture sets observed at four different localities within the Suesca Anticline, a core faulted anticline located in the Eastern Cordillera of Colombia. As expected, the area with higher density of fractures is the closest to the anticline crest. Nevertheless, high- density fracture corridors also exist at different positions within the fold. At each locality, areas with diverse fracture densities are observed. Spacing of fracture sets at high-density areas varies from 0.3 m to less than 1 meter, while spacing at low-density areas varies from 1 to 20 meters. Fractures in the steep forelimb are tilted, revealing that the main sets of fractures formed at an early stage of folding. Dominant sets of fractures are interpreted to have been formed normal to the maximum extensional (or minimum compressional) stress. Local variations in fracture density are explained by increase of the amount of shear displacement along the dominant fracture set. The orientation of the maximum compressive stress rotates across the structure, as can be deduced from the attitude of dominant fracture sets. A local stress perturbation, generated by shear displacements along the former joints, is the most likely explanation for the observed stress rotations. However, considering the complex history of deformation of the Eastern Cordillera, the possibility of joints related to a pre-folding state of stress can not be ruled out.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
